        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer: Batch Size Beats Drum Size
          </h2>

          <p>
            Almost every electric mixer is sold by drum volume, and almost nobody can fill the drum. A drum mixer works by tumbling the load as it turns at an angle, so the top of the drum has to stay empty or the mix slops out of the mouth and the paddles cannot lift the heavy stone to the top. The figure that actually tells you how much concrete you get per load is the batch, or mixing, capacity, and it usually sits around half the drum volume. Klutch is unusually honest about this: its 3.5 cubic foot mixer lists a 1.77 cubic foot mixing capacity.
          </p>

          <p>
            That gap is the whole buying decision. If you need about two bags at a time for fence posts, footings or a few steps, a 3.5 cubic foot wheelbarrow mixer is plenty. If you are pouring a small slab and want three or four 80 lb bags per load, you need a 5 to 6 cubic foot drum and a 3/4 hp motor. If you are mostly mixing thinset, mortar or self-leveler in a bucket, you do not need a drum at all; you need a paddle mixer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our overall pick is the Kushlan 600DD, a 6 cubic foot, 3/4 hp direct-drive wheelbarrow mixer that rolls to the form and dumps like a barrow. The smaller Kushlan 350DD is the one to buy, or rent, for post holes and patch jobs. The Yardmax YM0146 is the classic tilting steel-drum design for people who want a stand-mounted mixer that pours into a wheelbarrow. For bucket work, the DeWalt DCD130 cordless mixer drill and the Collomix Xo 1 R corded paddle mixer cover both ends of the budget.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We did not run these mixers on a job site. Our picks are built from the published specifications, owner's manuals, retailer spec sheets and the patterns that show up repeatedly in owner reports. Where sources disagreed, such as Kushlan's 600DD being listed at 280 lb of premix by one distributor and a little over 300 lb in a published hands-on review, we say so and use the more conservative figure for planning.
          </p>

          <p>
            For drum mixers we weighted three things above everything else. First, the usable batch: either a stated mixing capacity or a maximum load in pounds that we could translate into bags. Second, the drive: whether the motor is direct drive through a sealed gearbox or runs a belt, and whether a ring and pinion gear is exposed. Third, how the mixer gets concrete to the form, because a mixer that dumps where you need it saves a wheelbarrow trip on every single batch.
          </p>

          <p>
