        <div id="drum-vs-batch" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Drum Capacity vs Real Batch Size
          </h2>

          <p>
            The number on the box is the volume of the empty drum. It is a fair way to compare drums against each other, but it is not how much concrete comes out. In practice, a tilting drum mixer runs well at about half its drum volume, and a wide-mouth design may manage up to about 60 percent with a stiff mix. Push past that and the load spills during the tilt, the motor labors, and the gravel at the bottom never really gets folded through the paste.
          </p>

          <p>
            Weight limits tell a similar story. Kushlan's 350DD is listed as handling two 80 lb bags of premix, or roughly 1.2 cubic feet of finished concrete, from a 3.5 cubic foot drum. The 600DD is rated around 280 lb of premix, about three and a half 80 lb bags, from a 6 cubic foot drum. Measured as finished concrete, that is closer to 35 to 40 percent of the drum, because the bag yield figure is smaller than the drum space the wet, tumbling load needs.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Mixer</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Drum volume</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Stated batch or load</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Practical bags per batch (80 lb)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Kushlan 350DD</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3.5 cu ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Up to two 80 lb bags</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Klutch 49413</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3.5 cu ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1.77 cu ft mixing capacity</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Yardmax YM0146</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5.0 cu ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not stated as a separate figure</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3, owners commonly report</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Kushlan 600DD</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6.0 cu ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 280 lb of premix</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3, or four 60 lb bags</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The practical rule: divide the drum volume by two to estimate a comfortable batch in cubic feet, then divide that by 0.6 to get the number of 80 lb bags. A 3.5 cubic foot drum gives you about two bags, a 5 cubic foot drum about three and a 6 cubic foot drum three to four. If a maker publishes a mixing capacity or a maximum load, trust that over the rule of thumb.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="bags-per-batch" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Many Bags per Batch, and per Job
          </h2>

          <p>
            Bagged concrete yield is printed on the bag, and the common figures for standard concrete mix are about 0.6 cubic feet from an 80 lb bag, 0.45 from a 60 lb bag and 0.3 from a 40 lb bag. A cubic yard is 27 cubic feet, so it takes about 45 of the 80 lb bags to make one yard. That is the single most useful number on this page, because it tells you how many times you will load the mixer.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Bagged concrete, by the numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">0.6 cu ft</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Yield of one 80 lb bag</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">45</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">80 lb bags per cubic yard</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~50%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Usable share of drum volume</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">56</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">80 lb bags for a 10 x 10 ft slab, 4 in thick</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Take a 10 by 10 foot slab at four inches thick. That is about 33 cubic feet of concrete, or roughly 56 bags of the 80 lb size before waste. With a 3.5 cubic foot mixer doing two bags at a time, that is 28 batches. With a 6 cubic foot mixer doing three to four bags, it is 14 to 19. Either way you are moving well over two tons of bags by hand, which is why batch size, motor power and the ready-mix question below matter so much.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pick the mix before the mixer. Fast-setting mixes give you less working time per batch, so smaller batches are safer; high-strength and fiber-reinforced mixes are stiffer and work a small motor harder. Our guide to the <a href="/best-concrete-mix/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best concrete mix</a> compares the common bags by strength, set time and yield, and it is worth reading before you decide how many bags each load should hold.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="motor-drive" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Motor Size, Direct Drive and Belt Drive
          </h2>

          <p>
            Portable electric mixers generally run 1/2 to 3/4 hp motors on a standard 120 volt circuit. A 1/2 hp motor is fine for two bags in a 3.5 cubic foot drum; Klutch's 3.5 cubic foot model pairs a 1/2 hp, 5.3 amp motor with a 26 rpm drum. Once you move up to 5 and 6 cubic foot drums and loads of three bags or more, 3/4 hp is the sensible minimum, which is what Kushlan and Yardmax use on their larger models.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Direct drive or belt drive</h3>

          <p>
            A direct-drive mixer couples the motor to the drum through a gearbox, so there is no belt to stretch, slip or replace. Kushlan, Klutch and Yardmax all describe their current models as direct drive. A belt-drive mixer runs a belt from the motor to a pulley, which can slip under an overload and protect the motor, but it also needs tensioning and eventually a new belt, and a belt soaked in cement slurry wears quickly. For occasional homeowner use, direct drive is the lower-maintenance choice.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other drive detail is the gear on the drum. Classic tilting mixers such as the Yardmax turn the drum through a large ring gear and a small pinion. That design works well and is easy to repair, but it is an exposed pinch point, and splattered concrete in the teeth makes it wear faster. Yardmax encloses the ring and pinion with a guard, which we consider a requirement.
          </p>

          <p>
            Power supply matters more than the horsepower number. A mixer pulling five or more amps on a long, thin extension cord loses voltage and runs hot. Use the shortest heavy-gauge outdoor cord that reaches, plug into a GFCI-protected outlet, and keep the cord connection off wet ground. A motor that hums but will not turn a loaded drum is usually starved of voltage, not undersized.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="drum-vs-paddle" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Drum Mixer or Paddle Mixer?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A paddle mixer, sometimes called a mixing drill, is a low-speed, high-torque motor that spins a large spiral paddle inside a bucket or tub. It is the right tool for thinset tile mortar, grout, self-leveling underlayment, plaster, drywall mud and masonry mortar: smooth, fine-grained materials mixed a bucket at a time. Collomix rates its Xo 1 R for up to 40 liters, about 10.5 gallons, per mix, which is a generous mortar batch but a small amount of concrete.
          </p>

          <p>
            A drum mixer is the right tool for concrete with gravel in it. The tumbling action folds stone through the paste without the aggregate jamming the paddle, and the batch is large enough to make the work worthwhile. You can mix a single bag of concrete with a paddle in a heavy mixing tub, and plenty of people do, but coarse aggregate is hard on paddles, hard on wrists and slow compared with a drum.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not use an ordinary drill. Standard drill drivers spin too fast for mixing and are not built for the sustained torque of a dense bucket, and their clutches are designed for screws, not a paddle that catches. If you already own a big half-inch corded drill, it will survive the occasional bucket of joint compound, but for regular thinset or mortar work a dedicated mixer is cheaper than a burned-out drill. Our guides to <a href="/best-cordless-drills/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">cordless drills</a> and <a href="/hammer-drill-vs-drill-driver/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">hammer drills versus drill drivers</a> explain what ordinary drills are actually built for.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="rent-or-wheelbarrow" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. When to Rent, or Just Use a Wheelbarrow and Hoe
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy a mixer if you will use it on several projects, or if one project is big enough that renting by the day gets expensive. Rent one if you have a single weekend job. Big-box rental counters commonly stock small wheelbarrow mixers, and Home Depot lists the Kushlan 350DD as a rental item. Renting also lets you try the size before buying one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use a wheelbarrow and a hoe for anything under about five bags. A contractor wheelbarrow or a plastic mixing tub, a mortar hoe with holes in the blade and a bucket of measured water will mix one or two bags in a few minutes. There is no setup, no cord and almost nothing to clean. For setting a handful of fence posts, many people skip mixing entirely and use a fast-setting mix designed to be poured dry into the hole and soaked, following the directions on the bag.
          </p>

          <p>
            Order ready-mix above about one cubic yard. At 45 bags per yard, a 10 by 10 patio is more than a full day of lifting and mixing, and every batch has a slightly different water content, which shows up as color variation and weak spots. A ready-mix truck delivers one consistent load you can place and finish in one go. Many suppliers deliver short loads for an extra fee, and our breakdown of <a href="/concrete-patio-cost/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">concrete patio cost</a> shows where bagged concrete stops saving money.
          </p>

          <p>
            Weather can make the decision for you. Mixing bag after bag with a small machine stretches a pour over hours, and an afternoon storm can arrive before the last section is finished. If rain is in the forecast, read <a href="/can-you-pour-concrete-in-the-rain/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">whether you can pour concrete in the rain</a> first; a small mixer makes it easier to stop between batches, but it also makes the whole job last longer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="getting-a-good-mix" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Getting a Good Mix: Loading, Water and Cleanup
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start the drum turning before you load it, and put some of the water in first. Adding dry bags to an empty, stationary drum packs powder into the bottom corner, where the paddles never reach it. With the drum running, add part of the water, then the bags, then the remaining water a little at a time until the mix is workable. Most bag directions give a water range per bag; staying near the low end gives a stronger slab.
          </p>

          <p>
            Water is where most homemade concrete goes wrong. Every extra quart makes the mix easier to pour and weaker once cured, and it raises the risk of shrinkage cracks and a dusty surface. Aim for a mix that holds its shape like thick oatmeal, not one that runs off the shovel. Measure water into a marked bucket for the first batch and repeat that amount on every batch after it, so the whole pour is consistent.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mix each batch for a few minutes after the last material goes in, until the color is uniform and there are no dry pockets, then dump it promptly. Concrete left tumbling for a long time loses workability and tempts you to add water. Once the concrete is placed, the clock on finishing and curing starts; our guide to <a href="/how-long-does-concrete-take-to-cure/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">how long concrete takes to cure</a> covers when you can walk on it, drive on it and remove forms.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying by drum volume</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The drum size is not the batch size. Plan on about half the drum volume per load, or the maker's stated mixing capacity if it gives one. A 6 cubic foot mixer handles three or four bags, not ten, and overfilling it spills concrete and strains the motor.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Adding water until it pours easily</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Soupy concrete is easier to place and weaker once it cures, with more shrinkage cracking and a dusty surface. Stay near the low end of the water range on the bag, measure it, and use the same amount for every batch so the whole pour matches.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Dumping dry bags into a stopped drum</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Powder packs into the bottom of a stationary drum where the paddles cannot reach it, leaving dry clumps in the finished batch. Start the drum, add part of the water, then the bags, then the rest of the water gradually while the drum keeps turning.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a long, thin extension cord</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A mixer motor pulling five to ten amps on a long light-duty cord loses voltage, runs hot and may stall under a full load. Use the shortest heavy-gauge outdoor cord that reaches, plugged into a GFCI-protected outlet, and keep connections off wet ground.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mixing a full yard by hand with a small mixer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                At 45 bags of the 80 lb size per cubic yard, a slab means dozens of batches, hours of lifting and inconsistent concrete. Above about a yard, price ready-mix delivery before buying bags; a short-load fee is often cheaper than the extra labor.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size concrete mixer do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Match the batch to the job. A 3.5 cubic foot mixer handles about two 80 lb bags per load, which suits posts, footings and repairs. A 5 to 6 cubic foot mixer handles three or four bags, which suits steps and small slabs. Above about one cubic yard of concrete, ready-mix delivery is usually the better choice than any portable mixer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many bags of concrete can a 3.5 cu ft mixer hold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                About two 80 lb bags per batch. Kushlan rates its 3.5 cubic foot 350DD for up to two 80 lb bags, and Klutch lists a 1.77 cubic foot mixing capacity for its 3.5 cubic foot model. Loading more than that tends to spill during mixing and pouring and puts extra strain on a small motor.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a direct-drive or belt-drive concrete mixer better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For occasional home use, direct drive is the lower-maintenance option, because there is no belt to stretch, slip or replace. A belt drive can slip under overload, which protects the motor, but belts wear quickly in cement dust and slurry. Kushlan, Klutch and Yardmax all describe their current electric models as direct drive.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I mix concrete with a drill and paddle?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You can mix a single bag in a heavy tub with a dedicated paddle mixer, but gravel is hard on paddles and slow to blend. Paddle mixers are made for thinset, grout, mortar and self-leveler. An ordinary drill driver spins too fast and lacks the sustained torque, so it is likely to overheat or twist your wrist.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I rent or buy a cement mixer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Rent if you have one weekend project, and buy if you will mix concrete several times or have a job big enough that daily rental adds up. Big-box rental counters commonly stock small wheelbarrow mixers such as the Kushlan 350DD. Renting first also tells you whether the size suits your work.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should concrete mix in a mixer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Mix for a few minutes after the last material goes in, until the color is even and there are no dry pockets, then pour promptly. Follow the time on the bag if it gives one. Leaving concrete tumbling for a long time makes it stiffen, which tempts people to add water and weaken it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I clean a concrete mixer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Clean it immediately after the last batch. Put a few shovels of gravel and a bucket of water in the turning drum to scour the walls, dump it, then rinse with a hose. Avoid soaking the motor. Dry a steel drum before storing it to prevent rust, and never let concrete set inside the drum.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

